apter":"NPNFIX.26","count":3,"book":"Book VII","title":"The Apocalypse of John"}]},{"work":"anf01","title":"Ante-Nicene Fathers, Volume 1: The Apostolic Fathers with Justin Martyr and Irenaeus","count":46,"chapters":[{"chapter":"ANFXXX.68","count":3,"book":"Dialogue with Trypho","title":"Chapter LXVIII.—He complains of the obstinacy of Trypho; he answers his objection; he convicts the Jews of bad faith"},{"chapter":"ANFXXX.77","count":3,"book":"Dialogue with Trypho","title":"Chapter LXXVII.—He returns to explain the prophecy of Isaiah"},{"chapter":"ANFXXVIII.41","count":2,"book":"The First Apology","title":"Chapter XLI.—The crucifixion predicted"},{"chapter":"ANFXXVIII.51","count":2,"book":"The First Apology","title":"Chapter LI.—The majesty of Christ"},{"chapter":"ANFXXVIII.53","count":2,"book":"The First Apology","title":"Chapter LIII.—Summary of the prophecies"}]},{"work":"npnf102","title":"Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, Series 1, Volume 2: Augustine — The City of God and On Christian Doctrine","count":42,"chapters":[{"chapter":"NPNFXXX.1","count":5,"book":"The City of God","title":""},{"chapter":"NPNFXX.48","count":3,"book":"A parallel history of the ea","title":"That Haggai’s Prophecy, in Which He Said that the Glory of the House of God Would Be Greater Than that of the First Had Been, Was Really Fulfilled, Not in the Rebuilding of the Temple, But in the Church of Christ"},{"chapter":"NPNFXIX.3","count":2,"book":"The history of the city of G","title":"Of the Three-Fold Meaning of the Prophecies, Which are to Be Referred Now to the Earthly, Now to the Heavenly Jerusalem, and Now Again to Both"},{"chapter":"NPNFXIX.5","count":2,"book":"The history of the city of G","title":"Of Those Things Which a Man of God Spake by the Spirit to Eli the Priest, Signifying that the Priesthood Which Had Been Appointed According to Aaron Was to Be Taken Away"},{"chapter":"NPNFXIX.9","count":2,"book":"The history of the city of G","title":"How Like the Prophecy About Christ in the 89th Psalm is to the Things Promised in Nathan’s Prophecy in the Books of Samuel"}]},{"work":"anf03","title":"Ante-Nicene Fathers, Volume 3: Latin Christianity — Tertullian","count":41,"chapters":[{"chapter":"ANFXII.5","count":11,"book":"The Five Books Against Marci","title":"Book IV. In Which Tertullian Pursues His Argument. Jesus is the Christ of the Creator. He Derives His Proofs from St. Luke's Gospel; That Being the Only Historical Portion of the New Testament Partially Accepted by Marcion. This Book May Also Be Regarded as a Commentary on St. Luke. It Gives Remarkable Proof of Tertullian's Grasp of Scripture, and Proves that “The Old Testament is Not Contrary to the New.“ It Also Abounds in Striking Expositions of Scriptural Passages, Embracing Profound Views of Revelation, in Connection with the Nature of Man"},{"chapter":"ANFXII.4","count":9,"book":"The Five Books Against Marci","title":"Book III. Wherein Christ is shown to be the Son of God, Who created the world; to have been predicted by the prophets; to have taken human flesh like our own, by a real incarnation"},{"chapter":"ANFXII.6","count":6,"book":"The Five Books Against Marci","title":"Book V. Wherein Tertullian proves, with respect to St. Paul's epistles, what he had proved in the preceding book with respect to St. Luke's gospel. Far from being at variance, they were in perfect unison with the writings of the Old Testament, and therefore testified that the Creator was the only God, and that the Lord Jesus was his Christ. As in the preceding books, Tertullian supports his argument with profound reasoning, and many happy illustrations of Holy Scripture"},{"chapter":"ANFI.20","count":2,"book":"Apology","title":"Chapter XX"},{"chapter":"ANFVII.11","count":2,"book":"An Answer to the Jews","title":"Further Proofs, from Ezekiel. Summary of the Prophetic Argument Thus Far"}]},{"work":"anf04","title":"Ante-Nicene Fathers, Volume 4: Tertullian (Part Fourth), Minucius Felix, Commodian, Origen (Parts First an